  EML Stats : Correlation Analysis
══════════════════════════════════════════════

  Table               fixture corr group -- cohort = A
  Column X            x
  Column Y            y
  N                   4


  ── Pearson Correlation ─────────────────────
  Why: Measures linear association between two continuous variables.
  r                   0.9648		Very strong positive relationship (range: -1 to 1)
  R-squared           0.9308		The fitted model accounts for 93% of the variance in Y in this sample
  t                   5.185		The observed difference is 5.2 standard errors from zero
  df                  2		Observations (4) minus 2. Two parameters (slope, intercept) estimated.
  p                   .035		Statistically significant at the 5% level: if there were truly no effect, results at least this extreme would occur less than 5% of the time
  95% CI for r        [0.0505, 0.9993]
  Magnitude           large effect


  ── Spearman Correlation ────────────────────
  Why: Measures monotonic association — no normality assumption needed.
  rho                 1.0000		Very strong positive relationship (range: -1 to 1)
  t                   --undefined--		The observed difference is --undefined-- standard errors from zero
  df                  2		Observations (4) minus 2. Two parameters (slope, intercept) estimated.
  p                   < .001  (0)		Statistically significant: if there were truly no effect, results at least this extreme would occur less than 0.1% of the time
  Magnitude           large effect

  Conventions: quartiles R type 7 · SD & variance n-1
  · rank tests average tied ranks (tie-corrected).
